// --- Mbembe 4 · fourth freedom and liberal contradiction
m(4,"Core of struggles","According to Mbembe, what is increasingly the core of political struggles?","The capacity to decide who can move, settle, where and under what conditions",["The control of natural resources and energy supplies worldwide","The redistribution of income between rich and poor nations","The protection of national languages and cultures from outside"],"This is the standfirst of the essay.",1);
m(4,"21st-century trend","What trend does Mbembe see as the 21st century unfolds?","A renewed desire of citizens and states for tighter control of mobility",["A steady removal of borders in all the continents of the world","A decline of the nation-state in favour of regional blocs","A return to nomadic ways of life among younger generations"],"A drive toward enclosure and the dialectics of territorialisation and deterritorialisation.",1);
m(4,"Risk management","Which tool does Mbembe say increasingly governs mobilities?","Risk management techniques, including the biometric border",["Free-trade agreements and customs unions between regions","Cultural exchange programmes between universities and cities","Regional currency unions and common central banks"],"The biometric border extends into many realms of social life and also of the body.",2);
m(4,"Speed","Besides controlling bodies, what else do migration control policies aim to control?","Movement itself and its corollary, speed",["Language and religion of the people who migrate","Remittances and wages sent back by migrants","Family size and marriage among migrant households"],"“Not only through the control of bodies but the control of movement itself and its corollary, speed.”",2);
m(4,"Utopia","How does Mbembe define the concept of utopia?","That which has no borders, beginning with the imagination itself",["A perfect society that is protected by closed borders","A future that can be reached only through revolution","A society in which private property has been abolished"],"Borderlessness has been central to utopian traditions since their inception.",1);
m(4,"Utopian tension","The power of utopianism lies in instantiating the tension between…","Borderlessness, movement and place",["Freedom, equality and fraternity","State, market and society","Capital, labour and land"],"A tension that has marked social transformations in the modern era.",2);
m(4,"Racial violence","How does Mbembe say racial violence is increasingly encoded?","In the language of the border and of security",["In the language of culture and heritage","In the language of the market and competition","In the language of religion and tradition"],"Borders risk reinforcing the vulnerability of stigmatised, racially marked and disposable groups.",2);
m(4,"Premise","The borderless world of the fourth freedom is premised on…","A right of non-discrimination and circulatory, pendular migration",["A single world citizenship and one global government","The end of national sovereignty and of all taxation","A fair distribution of migration quotas between countries"],"Most people want to live where they belong, but to be able to come and go.",2);
m(4,"Unassimilable other","How does the classical liberal state configure people who restlessly move around, according to Kotef?","As an unassimilable other",["As temporary citizens","As natural allies of the state","As a source of national wealth"],"You cannot assimilate people who are constantly on the move.",2);
m(4,"Uncaptured","What was the biggest problem of the colonial state in Africa from the 19th century onwards?","Making sure that people stayed in the same place",["Finding enough soldiers to fill the colonial armies","Teaching the colonial languages in the schools of the colony","Building enough railways to cross the whole continent"],"People were constantly on the move and “uncaptured”.",2);
m(4,"Taxation","Which monopoly does Mbembe say everything else depends on?","The monopoly over taxation",["The monopoly over legitimate force","The monopoly over territory","The monopoly over currency"],"Sovereignty means capturing a people and a territory, with monopolies over territory, people and legitimate force, “and, very importantly, over taxation”.",3);
m(4,"Order","In the classical liberal model, order is about…","Securing the unequal ordering of property relations",["Guaranteeing equality of opportunity for all the citizens","Protecting the cultural rights of minority populations","Maximising the speed of migration across the borders"],"Security and freedom come to be defined as a right of exclusion.",3);
m(4,"South Africa","In which country does Mbembe say he has worked with a Home Affairs department on inter-African migrations?","South Africa",["Ghana","Cameroon","Kenya"],"He mentions work on recalibrating inter-African migrations with the Department of Home Affairs.",2);
m(4,"Who pays","Who has paid the heaviest price in the era of neoliberal abandonment, according to Mbembe?","Stigmatised, racially marked and disposable groups",["Highly skilled workers of the richest countries","Retired civil servants living on public pensions","Rural landowners with large agricultural estates"],"They have paid for the most expansive period of prison construction in human history.",2);
vf(4,"For Kotef, classical liberal thought treats every kind of movement as a form of freedom.",false,"False: some movements are configured as freedom, others are deemed improper and a threat.",2);
vf(4,"Hagar Kotef, whom Mbembe cites, teaches at the School of Oriental and African Studies in London.",true,"True: Mbembe describes her as an Israeli scholar teaching at SOAS in London.",2);
ty(4,"Concept by which liberal states reconcile freedom and security: “managed ___”",["mobility","managed mobility"],"Managed mobility.");
ty(4,"Number of core freedoms of movement in classical liberal thought (digit or word)",["3","three"],"Three: capital, goods and services. The fourth is the movement of persons.");

// --- Mbembe 5 · precolonial mobility, abode, carceral
m(5,"Long-distance trade","What does the tradition of long-distance trade show, according to Mbembe?","That circulation was fundamental to cultural, political and economic forms",["That precolonial states were isolated from each other by borders","That trade replaced religion as the unifying force of societies","That only the elites were ever allowed to travel long distances"],"Circulation was fundamental in the production of all those forms.",2);
m(5,"Survival","In conditions of crisis, what is the very condition of survival, according to Mbembe?","Being on the move",["Staying in place","Accumulating goods","Joining the state"],"“To stop is to run risks.”",1);
m(5,"Places and movement","In precolonial African conceptions of space, what mattered most?","The distribution of movement between places",["The exact location of towns and villages","The length of the roads between places","The size of each territory"],"Places were not described by points or lines; fixed spaces such as towns and villages did exist.",3);
m(5,"Geometry","What did movement lead to, in Dogon thinking, that mattered more than points, lines and surfaces?","Diversions, conversions and intersections",["Divisions, borders and hierarchies between peoples","Sacrifices, rituals and alliances between lineages","Walls, gates and crossroads between settlements"],"Points, lines and surfaces are cardinal references in Western geometrics.",3);
m(5,"Western archive","Why does Mbembe say the western archive does not help to imagine borderlessness?","It is premised on the crystallisation of the idea of a border",["It never studied Africa or the societies of the continent","It rejects the idea of mobility altogether, in every period","It is written only by colonial administrators and missionaries"],"We must harness the archives of the world at large, not only the western one.",2);
m(5,"Anthropocene","What does Mbembe say about the Anthropocene?","It is not new for these cosmogonies: people are unthinkable without nonhumans",["It was invented by Western scientists in the 20th century","It proves that borders are needed to protect nature","It concerns only the industrial societies of the 19th century"],"“We have always lived in that. It is not new.”",3);
m(5,"Peoplehood","Peoplehood in precolonial Africa included…","The living, the dead, the unborn, humans and non-humans",["Only the citizens registered by the state authorities","Only the members of the same ethnic group or clan","Only the living adults of a given village or town"],"Peoplehood, not nationhood.",2);
m(5,"Two debts","What kind of debt is “constitutive” in Mbembe's account?","A debt that spans the living, those before us and those who come after",["A debt owed to banks and other creditors, repaid with interest","A debt owed by colonised states to the colonial powers","A debt contracted between neighbouring states after wars"],"The other kind of debt is expropriatory, like debts to banks.",2);
m(5,"Loss of sovereignty","What automatically followed the loss of sovereignty under slavery or colonial rule?","The loss of the right to free movement",["The loss of religious traditions and of local languages","The end of long-distance trade between regions","The abolition of local taxation by the new rulers"],"Hence the dream of a redeemed African nation is linked to the right to come and go.",2);
m(5,"US example","Which of these is among the US examples of confinement that Mbembe lists?","The Pig Laws",["The Corn Laws","The Poor Laws","The Navigation Acts"],"Together with the Black Codes, vagrancy statutes and chain gangs.",2);
m(5,"Southern Africa","Which two Southern African examples of confinement does Mbembe cite?","Bantustans and labour reserves",["Kibbutzim and moshavim","Free ports and customs zones","Reserves of game and national parks"],"Along with the Code de l'indigénat and the carceral industrial complex in the US.",2);
m(5,"Entrapment","Why was entrapment “the precondition” for exploitation, according to Mbembe?","Exploiting African labour required keeping people confined",["It prevented the spread of diseases between regions of the colony","It helped to define the colonial languages of administration","It reduced the cost of maintaining the colonial armies"],"Struggles for emancipation were thus intertwined with the struggle to move freely.",2);
m(5,"Where published","Where does the essay appear in print?","In the latest print issue of the Chimurenga Chronic",["In the pages of the journal Foreign Affairs","In the monthly edition of Le Monde diplomatique","In a long-form feature of The New Yorker"],"It was also published on Africa Is a Country.",1);
vf(5,"Fixed geographical spaces such as towns and villages did exist in precolonial Africa, according to Mbembe.",true,"True: he does not want to exaggerate; they could even be origins of movement.",2);
vf(5,"Mbembe says the Ghanaian right of abode is a concept he has found in many other constitutions.",false,"False: he says he has not found it anywhere else.",2);
ty(5,"Precolonial Africa valued wealth in ___ over wealth in things",["people"],"Wealth in people.");
ty(5,"Mbembe's essay is about “a borderless ___” (one word)",["world"],"“The idea of a borderless world.”");

